# Personal agent instructions — Jakub Jezek

These instructions are loaded by Zed and goose in **every** project on this machine,
before any project-local instructions. Keep this short: it is prepended to every
request everywhere.

## Who I am

Jakub Jezek, developer working across the AYON pipeline ecosystem
(`github.com/ynput`), primarily on host-app addons (Nuke, Flame, Resolve, Hiero) and
workspace tooling.

## The multi-repo workspace

My work lives under a single workspace root, `__AYON_WORKSPACE_ROOT__`
(`$AYON_WORKSPACE_ROOT` if set — always prefer that variable over the literal path).
It is a personal git repo (`Jeza-Ynput-Dev-Workspace`) with many `ayon-*` sibling repos
checked out inside it, most of them gitignored clones of `github.com/ynput/*`.

- Workspace-level automation (Zed tasks, symlink/setup scripts) lives in
  `<root>/src/jeza_ynput_dev_workspace/` and is invoked as `uv run <entry-point>` — run
  it with `<root>` as the working directory even when the file you're editing is inside
  a different `ayon-*` repo.
- `ayon-backend`, `ayon-frontend`, `ayon-docker` are out of scope — never modify them.

## AYON house rules

- Addon anatomy: `client/`, `server/`, `package.py`, `create_package.py`.
- Pipeline contracts (Pyblish `order`, `hosts`, `families`, `productBaseType`,
  representations, creator/loader identifiers) are functional, not cosmetic — treat
  renames and signature changes as multi-file, cross-repo changes.
- Ruff is authoritative for Python style: 79 columns, 4 spaces, double quotes.
- Do not add a test framework to an addon that doesn't already have one.

## Shared AYON agentic instructions

If the current repository has a `.agents-main/` entry (a symlink to a local checkout of
`ayon-agentic-instructions`), read `.agents-main/AGENTS.md` first — it is the source of
truth for shared AYON conventions and the SDD constitution. If it does not exist, the
repository's own `AGENTS.md` (if present) is self-sufficient; fall back to the rules
above.
